WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.620
The sister of one of the victims in what's&nbsp;
likely the most high-profile murder case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.060
of all time is slamming true-crime shows for&nbsp;
not doing enough to include the perspectives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:13.800
of victims' families. She particularly&nbsp;
focused on one popular, recent example.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:19.380
Netflix's "Dahmer – Monster: The Jeffrey Dahmer&nbsp;
Story" has generated a lot of controversy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.820
with ethical concerns over its depiction&nbsp;
of events, details from the show's set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:28.860
and the showrunners reportedly not consulting&nbsp;
the families of Dahmer's victims. Yet the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.420
series also smashed a Netflix record by&nbsp;
becoming the streamer's biggest debut ever.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.900
Dramatizations of real-life&nbsp;
crimes really have to walk a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.480
fine line. While entirely fictional crime series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:44.280
like "Ozark" and "Good Girls," can get away with&nbsp;
some comic relief and outlandish storylines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:49.200
true crime has a responsibility to depict events&nbsp;
at least fairly close to how they really happened.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.540
According to Kim Goldman, "Monster" did not hit&nbsp;
that target. She is the sister of Ron Goldman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.540 --> 00:01:00.300
the man who was killed alongside Nicole Brown&nbsp;
Simpson in 1994. O.J. Simpson was tried and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:05.580
acquitted for both murders. Kim is now a writer&nbsp;
who also works with nonprofit organizations on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.080
a variety of causes. As the sister of a&nbsp;
high-profile murder victim, she did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.640
mince words in her criticism of "Monster" in an&nbsp;
interview with Entertainment Weekly. Goldman said,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.300
"Some article said Netflix is not&nbsp;
required to notify the families.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.200
And it bugged me! How s---ty is&nbsp;
that, that it doesn't even dawn on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.487
you from just a human space that you&nbsp;
would give someone a courtesy call?"

00:01:25.487 --> 00:01:30.840
Goldman added that when she and her family heard&nbsp;
about the FX series "The People v. O. J. Simpson:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.200
American Crime Story," they reached&nbsp;
out to ask what they could learn.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.100
"We asked if we could see an advanced&nbsp;
copy — we were told no. [...] I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.240
'Well, that's unfortunate.' It kind&nbsp;
of works against you. If you were to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.120
at least share it with me, then&nbsp;
I would be able to say, 'Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.080
they shared it with me, and this is what&nbsp;
I feel about it.' Missed opportunity."

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:54.840
"The People v. O.J. Simpson" and "Monster" both&nbsp;
had the same executive producer, Ryan Murphy.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.640
"You know, there's so many places that they took&nbsp;
creative liberties, and a part of me is thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:03.960
you know, 'Why do we need this show?' We had it&nbsp;
on television, gavel-to-gavel, for 10 months."

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:08.820
Goldman has raised the possibility of a law&nbsp;
requiring filmmakers to consult with families.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:13.680
She speculated as to the reasons why creative&nbsp;
teams would not contact families, saying,

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.280
"I would imagine [it's] because of the&nbsp;
pushback that a production company or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.840
network would receive if a family got wind [of&nbsp;
a project] and wanted some kind of involvement."

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.880
Goldman's issue with true crime shows and films&nbsp;
not consulting families isn't just about a lack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.140
of courtesy. Viewers have the expectation&nbsp;
that what they're viewing is a fictionalized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.340
but still largely accurate account of&nbsp;
events. But a well-rounded account of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.540
what happened would be difficult to portray&nbsp;
without consulting the families of victims.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.620
Goldman compared the idea of a true-crime&nbsp;
show that doesn't consult those families&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.720
to an "unauthorized" biography written without&nbsp;
the subject's permission or knowledge. She said,

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.460
"When or if I choose to read that book, now&nbsp;
I know the lens with which to view it. I feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:57.420
like that could be applied to these types of&nbsp;
shows, and then people can decide whether to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.800
watch it... I'm not trying to stop people from&nbsp;
[watching], but just maybe to have a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.180
sensitivity as you consume the show that it&nbsp;
wasn't at the pleasure of the victim's family."

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:11.520
Goldman also shared that she asked Jeffrey Toobin,&nbsp;
the author of the book that "Monster" was based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.960
on, about why family members of Dahmer's&nbsp;
victims were not consulted. She recalled:

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.400
"He said, 'Well, it wasn't victim-focused.'&nbsp;
And I'm like, 'Well, that's the problem.'"

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:21.540
Goldman added,

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:25.620
"I think it's changed a little bit over the&nbsp;
last couple of years, but most of the shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:29.760
are from the criminal's perspective, from the&nbsp;
law enforcement perspective, from the legal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.800
perspective. Very seldom do you often see it from&nbsp;
a victim's perspective. It paints a picture that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.940
victims and survivors are in the background,&nbsp;
you know, in a fetal position on the floor."

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:42.420
She said the experience of the&nbsp;
victims is just as important as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.360
those of the criminals and&nbsp;
the police. Goldman said:

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.260
"It's irresponsible to not in some way&nbsp;
include the families so that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:53.100
understand what they're experiencing and to&nbsp;
give a little bit more power back to them."

